    {
      "content": "{\"role\": \"B\", \"goal\": \"legal SIMD/vectorization and loop dependencies\", \"experiment\": \"c440c4bc12aa44c7-h01\", \"ordinal\": 2, \"integration_source_ids\": [], \"current_source_paths\": [\"src/model-view/primetest.c\", \"src/sources/primetest.c\"], \"context_as_data\": {\"sources\": [], \"interactions\": [], \"automatic_observations\": {\"baseline\": {\"experiment_id\": \"baseline-e1\", \"outcome\": {\"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.5, \"mean_seconds\": 0.044702706237634025}, \"raw_logs\": [{\"status\": \"failed\", \"code\": \"unavailable_diagnostic\"}, {\"status\": \"completed\", \"code\": \"check_passed\"}]}, \"selected_parent\": {\"experiment_id\": \"5816133714e64180\", \"role\": \"B\", \"hypothesis\": \"Deterministic Miller-Rabin should eliminate expensive floating-point divisor scans. Interleaving independent base-7 and base-61 chains after base-2 rejection should expose ARM64 instruction-level parallelism while preserving genuine loop-carried dependencies.\", \"outcome\": {\"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.9028236853492392, \"mean_seconds\": 0.004811619718869527}, \"raw_logs\": [{\"status\": \"failed\", \"code\": \"unavailable_diagnostic\"}, {\"status\": \"completed\", \"code\": \"check_passed\"}]}, \"selected_base_commit\": \"367f48b8ddb6ad74b33951fcdaab7f8687fcf5c8\", \"recent_own_role\": [{\"experiment_id\": \"5816133714e64180\", \"role\": \"B\", \"hypothesis\": \"Deterministic Miller-Rabin should eliminate expensive floating-point divisor scans. Interleaving independent base-7 and base-61 chains after base-2 rejection should expose ARM64 instruction-level parallelism while preserving genuine loop-carried dependencies.\", \"outcome\": {\"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.9028236853492392, \"mean_seconds\": 0.004811619718869527}, \"raw_logs\": [{\"status\": \"failed\", \"code\": \"unavailable_diagnostic\"}, {\"status\": \"completed\", \"code\": \"check_passed\"}]}], \"own_role_history\": [{\"experiment_id\": \"5816133714e64180\", \"status\": \"valid\", \"correct\": true, \"score\": 0.9028236853492392, \"mean_seconds\": 0.004811619718869527}], \"node_development\": {\"attempt\": 1, \"remaining_attempts\": 3, \"previous_attempts\": [], \"confirmation_data_available\": false}}, \"documents_as_data\": [], \"deadline\": 1792315754.293062, \"generation_deadline\": 1792315754.293062, \"protocol_id\": \"65515fc2587731cf1dc3da866686ff7ccaf616ff786ff65f6658ec624443eedd\", \"remaining_tokens\": 589824, \"official_statement_as_data\": \"Problem E\\nYet More Primes\\nPrime test is a common task in many applications, specially for security, cryptography,\\nand other interest areas. There are a number of techniques used to verify if a number is\\nprime, and the common problem is that all techniques consumes lots of time.\\nThe problem is easy to solve, giving the number to be tested and time enough to calculate.\\nBut a weird scientist took our numbers, and cut them in two halves, so we have ﬁrst to\\nre-arrange the numbers, looking for the primes. In this case, we have the certainty that all\\nthe numbers we have are primes, but the problem is how to re-order them\\nInput\\nIn the ﬁrst line, there is the numberP of prime numbers (1≤ P≤ 1000). Then, 2P lines\\nwith parts of the numbers to be tested, with no order. Prime numbers in this problem are\\ntaken as strings, so ﬁrst half and second half simply means an arbitrary cut of such string.\\nFor instance, prime number 504155039 can be cut in 5041 and 55039, or 504155 and 039\\nNote that second half numbers could begin with a lead zero, so you must take it into\\naccount. Of course, this doesn’t happen with the ﬁrst half.\\nThe input must be read from the standard input.\\nOutput\\nYou have to print the list of the P prime numbers, in ascendant order.\\nThe output must be written to the standard output.\\nExample\\nInput Output\\n4 1000213\\n50415 5041501\\n100 5575001\\n5041 504155039\\n55750 504155713\\n5039\\n01\\n0213\\n55713\\n\\n\\nThis experiment uses a reviewed finite certified input/output contract only. There are six private research cases and six distinct private confirmation cases. P is in {1,2,4,8,16,30}; the input lists two separately permuted groups of P decimal fragments. Left fragments have no leading zero and end in an even digit; right fragments have seven characters including a leading zero. Each original pair comes from a true prime and the multiset of every admissible prime concatenation equals the P source occurrences. Concatenations are at most 600971707; output is compared as exact bytes. The official example has an unresolved P/output-count contradiction. No claim is made for the whole contest domain or arbitrary fragment orders. The fixed benchmark is one member of the same certified finite set; no hidden values or expected outputs are supplied. Target: Linux ARM64, four CPU cores, 16 GiB RAM, zero GPUs. Compilation: GCC -O2 -std=c11 -Werror=uninitialized -Werror=maybe-uninitialized -lm.\", \"runtime_as_data\": {\"cpu_cores\": 4, \"OMP_NUM_THREADS\": 4, \"architecture\": \"ARM64\", \"candidate_GPU\": false, \"comparison\": \"Exact output bytes against official reference\"}, \"mother_model_as_data\": {\"phase\": \"preparation\", \"status\": \"complete\", \"guidance\": \"Research deterministic Miller-Rabin independently of A. Bases 2, 7, and 61 suffice throughout the certified numeric range. Implement complete strong-witness testing, correct small-number handling, and modular exponentiation using uint64_t products widened before multiplication; the candidate bound makes those products safe without floating point or 128-bit arithmetic. Compare modest small-prime prefilters against no additional prefilter, including their setup costs. Do not use probabilistic acceptance or an insufficient witness subset. Deliver a complete validated program with end-to-end measurements and a brief correctness justification for the witness range and arithmetic.\", \"analysis\": \"The only measured result is a correct baseline: mean 0.044702706237634025 seconds, score 0.5. The unavailable diagnostic provides no profiling evidence. Both supplied sources are identical. Their likely main cost is P² primality tests using floating-point remainder for successive integer divisors; repeated string construction is a secondary opportunity, not a measured bottleneck. Scope correctness to the reviewed certified contract, not the contradictory example or arbitrary contest inputs: at most 900 candidate pairs, seven-character right fragments, and concatenations no larger than 600971707. Preserve leading-zero significance, every prime occurrence, ascending order, and exact output bytes. These public structural bounds justify bounded arithmetic, not test-value specialization. Start single-threaded because the workload is small; four available cores do not establish a threading benefit. All proposed gains remain hypotheses until automatically measured.\", \"origin\": \"model output from this fresh run; no operator analysis\"}}, \"required_json_schema\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"hypothesis\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"transformations\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"sources_kept\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"uniqueItems\": true, \"maxItems\": 0}, \"files\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"path\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"content\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"path\", \"content\"]}}, \"notes\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"hypothesis\", \"transformations\", \"sources_kept\", \"files\", \"notes\"]}}\n\nCURRENT SOURCE AS DATA:\n\n--- BEGIN FILE src/model-view/primetest.c ---\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\n#define MAXNUM 100\n#define MAXSTR 50\n#define MAXRESULTS 1000\n\nchar firstHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar secondHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar strToTest[MAXSTR];\nlong int result[MAXRESULTS];\nint numPrimes=0;\n\nstatic inline uint32_t mulmod32(uint32_t a, uint32_t b, uint32_t n)\n{\n    return (uint32_t)(((uint64_t)a * (uint64_t)b) % (uint64_t)n);\n}\n\nstatic uint32_t powmod32(uint32_t base, uint32_t exponent, uint32_t n)\n{\n    uint32_t accumulator = 1u;\n    while (exponent != 0u) {\n        if ((exponent & 1u) != 0u)\n            accumulator = mulmod32(accumulator, base, n);\n        exponent >>= 1;\n        if (exponent != 0u)\n            base = mulmod32(base, base, n);\n    }\n    return accumulator;\n}\n\nint isprime(long int value)\n{\n    if (value < 2)\n        return 0;\n\n    /* Every certified candidate fits uint32_t. */\n    const uint32_t n = (uint32_t)value;\n    if ((n & 1u) == 0u)\n        return n == 2u;\n    if (n % 3u == 0u)\n        return n == 3u;\n    if (n % 5u == 0u)\n        return n == 5u;\n    if (n % 7u == 0u)\n        return n == 7u;\n    if (n % 11u == 0u)\n        return n == 11u;\n    if (n % 13u == 0u)\n        return n == 13u;\n    if (n < 17u * 17u)\n        return 1;\n\n    const uint32_t minus_one = n - 1u;\n    uint32_t d = minus_one;\n    unsigned int s = 0u;\n    do {\n        d >>= 1;\n        ++s;\n    } while ((d & 1u) == 0u);\n\n    /* Reject with base 2 before doing the remaining witness work. */\n    uint32_t x2 = powmod32(2u, d, n);\n    int pass2 = (x2 == 1u || x2 == minus_one);\n    for (unsigned int r = 1u; r < s && !pass2; ++r) {\n        x2 = mulmod32(x2, x2, n);\n        pass2 = (x2 == minus_one);\n    }\n    if (!pass2)\n        return 0;\n\n    /* Independent witness chains share only the exponent-bit schedule. */\n    uint32_t x7 = 1u;\n    uint32_t x61 = 1u;\n    uint32_t power7 = 7u;\n    uint32_t power61 = 61u;\n    uint32_t exponent = d;\n    while (exponent != 0u) {\n        if ((exponent & 1u) != 0u) {\n            x7 = mulmod32(x7, power7, n);\n            x61 = mulmod32(x61, power61, n);\n        }\n        exponent >>= 1;\n        if (exponent != 0u) {\n            power7 = mulmod32(power7, power7, n);\n            power61 = mulmod32(power61, power61, n);\n        }\n    }\n\n    int pass7 = (x7 == 1u || x7 == minus_one);\n    int pass61 = (x61 == 1u || x61 == minus_one);\n    for (unsigned int r = 1u; r < s && !(pass7 && pass61); ++r) {\n        x7 = mulmod32(x7, x7, n);\n        x61 = mulmod32(x61, x61, n);\n        /* Extra squarings cannot revoke an already passed witness. */\n        pass7 |= (x7 == minus_one);\n        pass61 |= (x61 == minus_one);\n    }\n    return pass7 && pass61;\n}\n\nvoid quicksort(long int *primes,int first,int last)\n{ int i, j, pivot;\n  long int temp;\n\n  if (first<last)\n     { pivot=first;\n       i=first;\n       j=last;\n       while(i<j)\n\t     { while (primes[i]<=primes[pivot]&&i<last)\n             i++;\n           while(primes[j]>primes[pivot])\n             j--;\n           if (i<j)\n\t\t      { temp=primes[i];\n                primes[i]=primes[j];\n                primes[j]=temp;\n              }\n         }\n       temp=primes[pivot];\n       primes[pivot]=primes[j];\n       primes[j]=temp;\n       quicksort(primes,first,j-1);\n       quicksort(primes,j+1,last);\n     }\n}\n\n\nint main(int argc,char** argv)\n{ FILE *primesFile;\n  int i=0,j=0,numResults=0;\n  long int primeToTest;\n  primesFile=stdin;\n  fscanf(primesFile,\"%d\\n\",&numPrimes);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",firstHalf[i]);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",secondHalf[i]);\n  fclose(primesFile);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n\t  for (j=0;j<numPrimes;j++)\n\t      { strcpy(strToTest,firstHalf[i]);\n\t        strcat(strToTest,secondHalf[j]);\n\t\t\tprimeToTest=atol(strToTest);\n\t\t\tif (isprime(primeToTest))\n\t\t\t   { result[numResults++]=primeToTest;\n\t\t\t   }\n\t      }\n  quicksort(result,0,numResults-1);\n  for (i=0;i<numResults;i++)\n      printf(\"%ld\\n\",result[i]);\n  return 0;\n}\n\n--- END FILE src/model-view/primetest.c ---\n\n--- BEGIN FILE src/sources/primetest.c ---\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\n#define MAXNUM 100\n#define MAXSTR 50\n#define MAXRESULTS 1000\n\nchar firstHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar secondHalf[MAXNUM][MAXSTR];\nchar strToTest[MAXSTR];\nlong int result[MAXRESULTS];\nint numPrimes=0;\n\nstatic inline uint32_t mulmod32(uint32_t a, uint32_t b, uint32_t n)\n{\n    return (uint32_t)(((uint64_t)a * (uint64_t)b) % (uint64_t)n);\n}\n\nstatic uint32_t powmod32(uint32_t base, uint32_t exponent, uint32_t n)\n{\n    uint32_t accumulator = 1u;\n    while (exponent != 0u) {\n        if ((exponent & 1u) != 0u)\n            accumulator = mulmod32(accumulator, base, n);\n        exponent >>= 1;\n        if (exponent != 0u)\n            base = mulmod32(base, base, n);\n    }\n    return accumulator;\n}\n\nint isprime(long int value)\n{\n    if (value < 2)\n        return 0;\n\n    /* Every certified candidate fits uint32_t. */\n    const uint32_t n = (uint32_t)value;\n    if ((n & 1u) == 0u)\n        return n == 2u;\n    if (n % 3u == 0u)\n        return n == 3u;\n    if (n % 5u == 0u)\n        return n == 5u;\n    if (n % 7u == 0u)\n        return n == 7u;\n    if (n % 11u == 0u)\n        return n == 11u;\n    if (n % 13u == 0u)\n        return n == 13u;\n    if (n < 17u * 17u)\n        return 1;\n\n    const uint32_t minus_one = n - 1u;\n    uint32_t d = minus_one;\n    unsigned int s = 0u;\n    do {\n        d >>= 1;\n        ++s;\n    } while ((d & 1u) == 0u);\n\n    /* Reject with base 2 before doing the remaining witness work. */\n    uint32_t x2 = powmod32(2u, d, n);\n    int pass2 = (x2 == 1u || x2 == minus_one);\n    for (unsigned int r = 1u; r < s && !pass2; ++r) {\n        x2 = mulmod32(x2, x2, n);\n        pass2 = (x2 == minus_one);\n    }\n    if (!pass2)\n        return 0;\n\n    /* Independent witness chains share only the exponent-bit schedule. */\n    uint32_t x7 = 1u;\n    uint32_t x61 = 1u;\n    uint32_t power7 = 7u;\n    uint32_t power61 = 61u;\n    uint32_t exponent = d;\n    while (exponent != 0u) {\n        if ((exponent & 1u) != 0u) {\n            x7 = mulmod32(x7, power7, n);\n            x61 = mulmod32(x61, power61, n);\n        }\n        exponent >>= 1;\n        if (exponent != 0u) {\n            power7 = mulmod32(power7, power7, n);\n            power61 = mulmod32(power61, power61, n);\n        }\n    }\n\n    int pass7 = (x7 == 1u || x7 == minus_one);\n    int pass61 = (x61 == 1u || x61 == minus_one);\n    for (unsigned int r = 1u; r < s && !(pass7 && pass61); ++r) {\n        x7 = mulmod32(x7, x7, n);\n        x61 = mulmod32(x61, x61, n);\n        /* Extra squarings cannot revoke an already passed witness. */\n        pass7 |= (x7 == minus_one);\n        pass61 |= (x61 == minus_one);\n    }\n    return pass7 && pass61;\n}\n\nvoid quicksort(long int *primes,int first,int last)\n{ int i, j, pivot;\n  long int temp;\n\n  if (first<last)\n     { pivot=first;\n       i=first;\n       j=last;\n       while(i<j)\n\t     { while (primes[i]<=primes[pivot]&&i<last)\n             i++;\n           while(primes[j]>primes[pivot])\n             j--;\n           if (i<j)\n\t\t      { temp=primes[i];\n                primes[i]=primes[j];\n                primes[j]=temp;\n              }\n         }\n       temp=primes[pivot];\n       primes[pivot]=primes[j];\n       primes[j]=temp;\n       quicksort(primes,first,j-1);\n       quicksort(primes,j+1,last);\n     }\n}\n\n\nint main(int argc,char** argv)\n{ FILE *primesFile;\n  int i=0,j=0,numResults=0;\n  long int primeToTest;\n  primesFile=stdin;\n  fscanf(primesFile,\"%d\\n\",&numPrimes);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",firstHalf[i]);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n      fscanf(primesFile,\"%s\\n\",secondHalf[i]);\n  fclose(primesFile);\n  for (i=0;i<numPrimes;i++)\n\t  for (j=0;j<numPrimes;j++)\n\t      { strcpy(strToTest,firstHalf[i]);\n\t        strcat(strToTest,secondHalf[j]);\n\t\t\tprimeToTest=atol(strToTest);\n\t\t\tif (isprime(primeToTest))\n\t\t\t   { result[numResults++]=primeToTest;\n\t\t\t   }\n\t      }\n  quicksort(result,0,numResults-1);\n  for (i=0;i<numResults;i++)\n      printf(\"%ld\\n\",result[i]);\n  return 0;\n}\n\n--- END FILE src/sources/primetest.c ---\n",
